  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_rhj3PgmSSbjd2v4S",
   "name": "Factorising Expressions",
   "definition": "",
   "video_id": "867649113"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Factorising expressions"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is meant by factorising expressions?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Many **expressions** in mathematics are written as a **sum** of terms\n\n  - e.g.  $x^{2}+6x-16$ is the **sum** of **three** the terms $x^{2}$, $6x$ and $-16$\n- Many **expressions** are written as a **product **of factors\n\n  - e.g.  $(x+8)(x-2)$ is the **product** of the **two** (linear) **factors** $x+8$ and $x-2$\n- **Factorising** is the process of rewriting the **sum** of **terms** as a **product** of **factors**\n\n  - The other way round is **expanding**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I factorise an expression?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This will depend on the nature of the expression you are dealing with\n- In **all** cases the first thing to consider is if there is a factor (number and/or letter) of all terms in the expression\n\n  - e.g. $2x^{3}+4x^{2}-8x=2x(x^{2}+2x-4)$\n- A **quadratic** expression may be able to be **factorised** into **two** **linear** **factors**\n\n  - Look out for **special** cases\n  \n    - **No** **constant** term: $x^{2}+5x=x(x+5)$\n    - **Difference** **of** **two** **squares** (no *x*  term and constant is square): $x^{2}-36=(x-6)(x+6)$\n    - **Perfect squares**: `x squared plus 8 x plus 16 equals open parentheses x plus 4 close parentheses open parentheses x plus 4 close parentheses equals open parentheses x plus 4 close parentheses squared`\n    - **‘Hidden’** **quadratics: **`3 to the power of 2 x end exponent minus 12 cross times 3 to the power of x plus 27 equals open parentheses 3 to the power of x close parentheses squared minus 12 open parentheses 3 to the power of x close parentheses plus 27 equals left parenthesis 3 to the power of x minus 3 right parenthesis left parenthesis 3 to the power of x minus 9 right parenthesis`\n    - **More than one variable: **$x^{2}-y^{2}=(x-y)(x+y)$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A **cubic** expression (at this level) will **not** contain a **constant** term\n\n  - This means will $x$ be a **factor** (and there might be a number as a factor too)\n  - The remaining **expression** will be a **quadratic**\n  \n    - this **quadratic** may also be able to be **factorised**\n    - e.g. `6 x cubed plus 3 x squared minus 9 x equals 3 x open parentheses 2 x squared plus x minus 3 close parentheses equals 3 x open parentheses 2 x plus 3 close parentheses open parentheses x minus 1 close parentheses`"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I factorise harder quadratics?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- There are many shortcuts to factorise quadratic expressions, but they often only apply under certain conditions (such as when *a* = 1)\n\n  - the method below works for **any** quadratic expression\n  - it is most useful when the **coefficient** of the $x^{2}$ term is **greater** than 1 (and **not** **prime**)\n- Follow the steps:\n\n  - **STEP 1** Starting with $ax^{2}+bx+c$ find the product $ac$\n  \n    - For example: for $6x^{2}+7x-3$ $ac=6\\times-3=-18$\n  - **STEP 2** Find two numbers *m* & *n* whose **product** is $ac$ and **sum** is $b$\n  \n    - For example: $9\\times-2=-18=ac$ & $9+(-2)=7=b$\n    - So `m equals 9 space & space n equals negative 2`\n  - **STEP 3** Split the $bx$ term into $mx+nx$\n  \n    - For example: $6x^{2}-2x+9x-3$\n  - **STEP 4** Factorise the first two terms and the last two terms\n  \n    - For example: `2 x open parentheses 3 x minus 1 close parentheses plus 3 open parentheses 3 x minus 1 close parentheses`\n  - **STEP 5** Factorise once more for the final answer\n  \n    - For example: $(3x-1)(2x+3)$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- If *a* and/or *c* are **prime**, factorising can be done “**by** **inspection”**\n\n  - For example: the only way to split (prime) 3 into factors would be 3 and 1"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Why does the 'ac' method work?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Suppose `a x squared plus b x plus c identical to open parentheses p x plus r close parentheses open parentheses q x plus s close parentheses`\n\n  - then expanding and simplifying gives\n  \n    - `a x squared plus b x plus c identical to blank p q x squared plus p s x plus q r x plus r s identical to p q x squared plus open parentheses p s plus q r close parentheses x plus r s`"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- By **comparing** **coefficients**\n\n  - $a=pq$\n  - $b=ps+qr$\n  - $c=rs$\n- Let $m=ps$ and $n=qr$ then:\n\n  - $m+n=ps+qr=b$\n  - $m\\timesn=psqr=ac$\n  - Therefore these are the two numbers whose **product** is *ac *and **sum** is *b*"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "![1-5-1-ial-fig1-we-solution-fact](assets/3694e8395e85-1-5-1-ial-fig1-we-solution-fact.png)"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Do use your tried and tested shortcuts for factorising quadratics\n\n  - We’ve explained it in full above to help you understand the process rather than to learn ‘tricks’\n- You **don't need to learn** why the '*ac*' method works - but we thought you might think that the algebra is cool"
  }
 ]
}